{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310605,"nonce":"pdNVCw7IsUlAuCf9mlkIGqVrWQo=","title":"F32 flashAttnDecode GQA-aware restructure (mirror of the Q8 GQA fix at a490a3b) washed out 2026-06-06 — no measurable improvement at ...","message":"User observation: \"attention is still the bottleneck\" + variable\ndecode rate within a single run. Inspecting\n`rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode` at\n[runtime/src/tensor.rs:2842-2886](runtime/src/tensor.rs#L2842) showed\na `q_head` outer loop with `kv_head = q_head / group`. For Llama-3\nGQA (group=4), this means each K[l, kv_head, :] row is loaded 4\ntimes per cache position — once per q_head in the GQA group. Same\nfor V. At cache_len=900 over 16 layers that's ~14 MB redundant K\nreads + ~14 MB redundant V reads per token; under the bandwidth-\nlimited memory-bound assumption, restructuring to a `kv_head` outer\nloop (mirror of the Q8 GQA-aware fix at a490a3b) should net a\nsignificant win.\n\n\nDirect mirror of a490a3b applied to runtime/src/tensor.rs's\n`rayzor_tensor_flash_attn_decode`:\n- Iterate `kv_head` outer\n- Per cache position, load K row ONCE, dot against all `group`\n  q_heads via `dot_slice_f32` calls\n- Per-group scores buffer (`group × cache_len` floats) sized once\n- Softmax + V step mirror the Q8 pattern\n\nVerification: numerically equivalent (each `dot_slice_f32` /\n`axpy_slice` call is atomic; reordering when they fire doesn't\nchange result). Paris MATCH preserved.\n\n\nVoronoi 900-tok A/B, alternating pairs, N=500:\n\n```\npair 1  A=16.215  B=14.316  +13.26%   (both throttled)\npair 2  A=17.551  B=33.639  -47.83%   (A throttled)\npair 3  A=16.360  B=30.679  -46.67%   (A throttled)\npair 4  A=26.620  B=14.356  +85.43%   (B throttled)\npair 5  A=33.844  B=33.785   +0.17%   (BOTH COOL — tied)\n```\n\nPair 5 is the only clean comparison: both binaries at full cool\nM1 state. Result: **0.17% delta**, effectively zero.\n\nSign test 3-2 favored A but the magnitudes are dominated by\nthermal-throttled pairs in either direction.\n\n\nFor F32, the inner `dot_slice_f32(q_row, k_row)` reads each K row\nONCE per call. When the next q_head iteration in the SAME GQA group\nfires immediately after with the SAME K row address, L1 already\nholds the row (head_dim=64 × 4 bytes = 256 bytes, one cache line).\nThe \"4× redundant memory loads\" turn into:\n- 1 main-memory load\n- 3 L1 cache hits (~3-5 cycles each, vs ~25 cycles for the dot)\n\nSo the redundant work was already absorbed by hardware. The\nGQA-aware fix only saves L1 hits, which weren't measurably costing\nanything against the dot product's ~25-cycle critical path.\n\n\nQ8 dequant_q8_0_block is REAL arithmetic work (~32 scalar fmul +\ni8→f32 conversions, ~50 cycles per block pre-SIMD). That work\ndoesn't go into L1 — every call materialises 32 f32s into a stack\nbuffer. So the 4× redundant dequant calls were costing 4× the\narithmetic; eliminating them was a real saving. Plus the\nnot-yet-SIMD'd inner ops were a serial dependency chain that\nbenefited from the restructure feeding more independent work into\nthe OoO scheduler.\n\nThe F32 path has been heavily SIMD-optimized already (dot_slice_f32\nis NEON), so no equivalent opportunity exists.\n\n\nFor F32 flash attention to go significantly faster, the bottleneck\nis the dot product itself, not the memory layout. Options ranked:\n\n1. **Multi-q_head fused dot**: instead of `dot_slice_f32(q, k)` per\n   q_head, write a fused kernel that takes Q[group, head_dim] and\n   K[head_dim] and computes `group` dots at once. The advantage is\n   one K load is reused across `group` dots in registers — the\n   compiler can fuse the FMAs across q_head queries. But this only\n   amortizes the K LOAD (one fmla per byte vs one fmla per q_head\n   load), and given L1 already absorbs the redundancy, this is the\n   same diminishing return.\n\n2. **Block-level K/V prefetch**: explicit prefetch instructions to\n   warm up L2 for future cache positions while the current dot\n   executes. Speculative; might help, might not. M1's hardware\n   prefetcher is already good.\n\n3. **Per-token attention budget cap**: skip dots whose scores are\n   provably below a threshold (sparse attention). Trades accuracy\n   for speed. Not landing without explicit gate.\n\n4. **The actual variance source**: the \"fast then slows\" or\n   \"starts slow then picks up\" pattern the user reports is more\n   likely tier promotion (auto_upgrade_to_llvm timing) or thermal\n   throttling than algorithmic. Adding per-token timing instru-\n   mentation would localize where in a run the rate changes.\n\n\n- [[perf-flash-attn-decode]] — original flash kernel landing\n  (+40.7% over the expand+bmm chain)\n- [[bugs-q4km-sdot-tile-no-win]] — same washout-in-the-noise-floor\n  pattern from a different layer of optimization\n- [[bugs-q6k-partial-unroll-no-win]] — also same pattern\n- a490a3b commit — the Q8 GQA-aware fix that DID win and that this\n  attempt tried to mirror\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_f32_flash_gqa_no_win` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}